yabasanshiro-atlas.patch, ours (1.1.0): the texture atlas in the CPU's own
memory, uploaded from there.

YabaSanshiro's OpenGL renderer fills a texture atlas again every frame. It
was a pixel buffer, mapped for the CPU to fill and then uploaded up to its
last row with glTexSubImage2D from the bound buffer (YglTmPush). On Panfrost
that upload is done on the CPU, reading the buffer back from uncached GPU
memory: 15.3 ms of Virtua Fighter 2's 54 ms frame on a GKD Pixel 2, timed in
the core (1.1.0-rc.7, 2026-11-20). Two textures in turn and fresh storage
before each upload both left it at 15.3 ms, so it was not a wait for the GPU.
Now the atlas is a malloc'd block and the upload reads it from cached memory
with no buffer bound: 5.1 ms, and the game 0.30x -> 0.35x. Nothing else reads
the atlas (it is filled again every frame), so the pixel buffers go: YglTMInit
mallocs, YglTmPull has nothing to map, YglTMRealloc grows the block and keeps
what is in it byte for byte as the buffer copy did, YglTMDeInit frees it.
git apply skips this preamble.

